      <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
        Frequently Asked Questions: Significant Figures
      </h3>

      <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.85rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What are the five golden rules of significant figures?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            1. All non-zero digits are always significant. 2. Captive zeros trapped between non-zero digits are always significant. 3. Leading zeros before the first non-zero digit are never significant. 4. Trailing zeros after a decimal point are significant. 5. In scientific notation, only the mantissa digits determine significant figures.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How many significant figures are in 100 versus 100.?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            The number 100 has 1 significant figure because trailing zeros without an explicit decimal point are treated as ambiguous scale placeholders. In contrast, 100. (with a terminal decimal point) has 3 significant figures, indicating that the measurement is precise down to the units position.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">Why do addition and multiplication use different significant figure rules?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Addition and subtraction propagate absolute uncertainty, which means the final result is limited by the measurement with the fewest decimal places (least decimal precision). Multiplication and division propagate relative percentage uncertainty, meaning the final answer is limited by the measurement with the fewest total significant figures.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How do you handle significant figures in mixed multi-step calculations?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            In mixed multi-step operations following PEMDAS, follow intermediate rules without rounding early to avoid rounding bias. Keep track of the limiting decimal places in addition/subtraction steps, and then use that intermediate precision count to determine the limiting significant figures in subsequent multiplication/division steps.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">Are exact numbers subject to significant figure rules?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            No. Exact numbers derived from counting discrete objects (e.g., 12 test tubes) or defined unit conversions (e.g., exactly 1000 mL = 1 L, or 1 inch = 2.54 cm) possess an infinite number of significant figures and never limit the precision of a calculation.
          </p>
        </details>
